# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# KubeTower — configuration. Copy this file to .env and adapt it.
#
# Two readers, and the difference matters on almost every line:
#
#   * the binary reads every KT_* variable directly, whether it was started by
#     `go run ./cmd/kubetower`, by a double-click, or by Docker;
#   * Docker Compose reads this file from the directory it runs in, and forwards
#     to the container only what `compose.dev.yaml` names in its `environment:`
#     block. A variable absent from that block never arrives, however carefully
#     it is written here.
#
# And a third, which reads three lines and no more: `task dev`
# and `task password` take KT_PASSWORD_HASH, KT_SESSION_SECRET and KT_STATE_DIR
# from `.env` (`internal/devenv`), never over your shell's own value. Nothing
# else here reaches the desktop process — KT_FORWARD_PORTS below above all,
# which would bind every port-forward on 0.0.0.0 there.
#
# **For the desktop loop you do not need to copy this file at all.** Run
#
#   task dev:password -- <word>        (or with no word, to be asked)
#
# once per clone. It creates `.env` if it is absent, or edits it in place, and
# writes three variables: KT_PASSWORD_HASH always, KT_SESSION_SECRET and
# KT_STATE_DIR only when they are empty. Each is described below where it
# belongs, and `docs/DEVELOPMENT.md` §2 opens on it.
#
# `internal/launch/envwiring_test.go` is the gate on both halves: every KT_
# variable the code reads is listed here, and every one is forwarded by each
# compose file or carries a written reason for not being.
#
# Nothing in this file is a secret. `.env` is — it holds the password hash and
# the session key — and `.dockerignore` keeps it out of the build context for
# that reason.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Host directories mounted into the container
#
# Container-only. Running the binary on the host needs none of this: it reads
# your own kubeconfig and your own AWS configuration where they already are.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# **Derive the home rather than typing it.** `${USERPROFILE}` on Windows,
# `${HOME}` elsewhere — Compose interpolates these from the shell that runs it,
# so one `.env` works on every machine and there is nothing to keep in step. A
# path nobody types cannot drift between two spellings of one home directory.
#
# The `:?` is not decoration. Unset, the variable resolves to the empty string
# and Compose mounts `/.kube` — a directory that exists, is empty, and is
# indistinguishable from a kubeconfig with no clusters in it. An empty answer
# and a failed answer are different answers, and that one arrives as a mount.
# With the `:?`, Compose refuses and prints the sentence.
#
# Write forward slashes on Windows if you do write a literal, never backslashes.
#
#   ~/.kube is mounted **read-only**. KubeTower reads the file that describes
#   your clusters; it never writes it, and a console holding administrator
#   credentials has no business being able to rewrite where they point.
#   ~/.aws is mounted read-write on purpose — the AWS CLI renews the SSO token
#   inside ~/.aws/sso/cache, and read-only it fails the moment one expires and
#   every EKS cluster goes dark at once.
HOST_KUBE_DIR=${USERPROFILE:?USERPROFILE is unset - set HOST_KUBE_DIR to your home directory explicitly}/.kube
HOST_AWS_DIR=${USERPROFILE:?USERPROFILE is unset - set HOST_AWS_DIR to your home directory explicitly}/.aws


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Where it listens
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The host side of the published mapping, on 127.0.0.1 only. Compose maps it
# onto the container's fixed 5823; **the process itself never reads KT_PORT**,
# which is why `envwiring_test.go` lists it as deliberately not forwarded.
KT_PORT=5823

# The address the binary binds. It defaults to 127.0.0.1:5823 — loopback, so
# starting it on a laptop does not put a console holding production credentials
# onto the local network. Leave it unset on the host.
#
# `compose.dev.yaml` and the image both set `:5823`, and that is correct there
# and only there: Docker cannot route a published port to a socket bound to the
# container's own loopback, so a listener on 127.0.0.1 inside the container is
# reachable from nothing. The mapping exists, the port shows in `docker compose
# ps`, and a dial from the host is refused.
# KT_LISTEN=127.0.0.1:5823

# The ports a port-forward may listen on, published by `compose.dev.yaml` and
# read by the binary, which allocates each forward's local port out of the range.
# Both from one variable, because a kernel-chosen port inside the container's
# network namespace could never be dialled from the host browser.
#
# **Leave it unset when you run the binary on your own machine.** There,
# 127.0.0.1 is the same loopback in the process and in the browser, the kernel
# picks the port as it always did, and there is nothing to publish. Setting it
# on the host makes every forward listen on 0.0.0.0 instead, which is what a
# published port needs and what a laptop on a shared network does not want.
#
# A forward is a raw TCP socket into a production workload with no password of
# its own. Published on the host loopback, so nothing off this machine can reach
# one — and every local account on it can, for as long as it is open.
#
# `task dev` never takes it from this file, which is what lets one `.env` serve
# the container and the desktop loop; `task dev:password` says so when it finds
# it. Sourcing this file into a shell that runs the binary does not have that
# protection.
KT_FORWARD_PORTS=30000-30031


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The front door
#
# **There is no way to start this process without a password, and that is not a
# setting.** `internal/auth.New` refuses an empty hash and `cmd/kubetower` calls
# `log.Fatalf` on the refusal. Nothing in this file turns that off, and nothing
# should ever be added that does: this process holds administrator credentials
# for production clusters, so starting without a front door is not "less safe",
# it is "anyone who can reach this socket has production".
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The login name. Defaults to `admin` when unset.
KT_USER=admin

# The bcrypt hash of the password — **never the password**.
#
# **Empty is a supported value and it does not mean "no password".** With no
# hash in the environment the binary falls back to `credentials.json` in
# KT_STATE_DIR, and on a first run it draws a 20-character password from
# crypto/rand, shows it once, and keeps only its bcrypt hash. It is shown three
# ways at once because a launcher can throw the first two away: the log, a box
# on Windows, and `PASSWORD-ONCE.txt` in the state directory, which deletes
# itself at the first successful login. After that nothing can give it back —
# `kubetower --reset-password` draws another.
#
# In a container the state directory is a named volume, so the same mechanism
# works there: read the drawn password out of `docker compose logs`. The
# environment is consulted first and wins, so a stale credentials file in a
# volume can never override what a deployment set.
#
# Set it here when a deployment must arrive already knowing its password.
# **Every `$` must be doubled**: Compose interpolates variables inside `.env`
# values, and a bcrypt hash is `$2a$14$<salt><digest>` — three variable
# references as far as Compose is concerned, each replaced by nothing, so a
# 60-character hash reaches the process as a 51-character stump and no password
# can match. Written by hand it is `$$2a$$14$$...`. The trap is salt-dependent,
# so the same procedure works on one laptop and locks you out on the next:
# check what arrived rather than what you wrote.
#
#   docker compose -f compose.dev.yaml exec kubetower sh -c 'echo ${#KT_PASSWORD_HASH}'
#
# must print 60.
#
# **Or single-quote it, which is what `task dev:password` writes:**
# `KT_PASSWORD_HASH='$2a$10$...'`. Compose takes a single-quoted value
# literally, and so does Task's loader, so one line serves the container and
# `task dev` alike with no doubling. Checked with `docker compose config`: the
# single-quoted hash arrives whole, the same hash unquoted arrives
# as `$2a$10`.
#
# **For development, `task dev:password -- <word>` fills this line** with the
# hash of a word you chose, so you never need the drawn password at all, and can
# type the same one tomorrow. That is not a default: the word is yours and only
# its hash is written. `task password` refuses while this is set, because a
# drawn password would never be asked for.
KT_PASSWORD_HASH=

# The session signing key, at least 32 bytes. Left empty, one is drawn at every
# start and every session dies with the process — the log says so. On the host
# the key persisted in `credentials.json` is used instead, so closing and
# reopening the binary does not sign you out; in a container that file lives in
# the state volume and has the same effect.
#
# Rotating this is the only logout-everywhere this design has: the token is
# stateless, so signing out drops a cookie and revokes nothing. **Changing
# KT_PASSWORD_HASH above also ends every session the shared password opened**
# (and every launch-code session), because their key is derived from this and
# the hash together; single sign-on sessions are not touched by it.
#
# `task dev:password` draws one here when it is empty or shorter than 32
# characters (the console ignores a shorter one), because a hash set above
# turns the persisted key off: with KT_PASSWORD_HASH and no KT_SESSION_SECRET,
# every start draws a key, and `task dev` restarts the Go half on every saved
# .go file — every save would sign you out.
KT_SESSION_SECRET=

# How long the console may go **unused** before it asks again. It slides: any
# request from a live session pushes the deadline out, so a session in use does
# not run out from under you. Default 30 days.
#
# The name says TTL and the meaning is an idle window. It was not renamed
# deliberately — renaming it would silently widen every installation that had
# shortened it on purpose.
#
# The cookie is only rewritten once it has fallen an hour behind, so the window
# you can rely on is this minus an hour. That is what stops a two-second table
# refresh writing 1 800 Set-Cookie headers an hour.
KT_SESSION_TTL=720h

# The ceiling: how long a session may live counted from the login, however much
# it is used. Default 90 days.
#
# This is the one that bounds a **stolen** cookie, and it is why the idle window
# is not the whole answer: a stolen cookie is used, and being used is what keeps
# an idle window open forever. Shortening it takes effect on cookies already in
# a browser.
KT_SESSION_MAX=2160h

# How long one followed log may run before the pane says it stopped and asks the
# person to reopen it: a Go duration, from 1m to 24h. It is the only bound on a
# follow whose tab crashed without closing its connection. Default 2h on the
# desktop (and the containerised desktop), 30m under --incluster, where the
# sixteen follows are every person's. A value that is not a duration, or is out
# of range, refuses start-up.
# KT_LOG_FOLLOW_MAX=2h

# The proxies in front of the console whose X-Forwarded-For is believed when a
# password attempt is counted and logged: addresses or CIDR prefixes, separated
# by commas. Each client address may make ten attempts at once and one more
# every six seconds; past that the sign-in answers 429 without checking the
# password. Empty believes nobody, and the connecting address is the client -
# behind an ingress that is the ingress controller's pod for every person, so
# set it to the controller's pod range there. A value that does not parse stops
# the console at start.
KT_TRUSTED_PROXIES=


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Single sign-on (OpenID Connect)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Empty issuer means no identity provider, and every variable below it is
# ignored. With one, a person signs in through the provider and gets a session
# of their own: signing one person out does not sign anybody else out.
#
# **The server profile only** (--incluster). With an issuer and no
# --incluster the process refuses to start: the desktop profile serves a local
# shell, a node shell and the assistant as this process, and an identity
# provider would hand them to every person it accepts. compose.dev.yaml runs
# the desktop profile and does not forward these; the chart sets them.
#
# **There is no allow-list of users or groups.** Every person the provider
# signs in gets a session. With a broad provider - Google, a whole tenant,
# GitHub through Dex without an organization restriction - that can mean
# anyone, so restrict who may sign in at the provider.
#
# **Setting an issuer removes the shared password** - its routes are not
# registered and no password is drawn - unless KT_AUTH_LOCAL_ACCOUNT=true keeps
# it. An unreachable provider does not stop the console: it starts, keeps
# asking, and the sign-in page says the provider is unavailable and why.
#
# **It decides who you are, never what you may do.** That is the cluster's RBAC,
# asked per person: before every write, exec and reveal the console
# itself makes, it sends a SubjectAccessReview naming the person and their
# groups (plus system:authenticated), sends nothing when the answer is no, and
# writes the decision on the `write:` line. **What a person reads is asked the
# same way**: a list across every namespace shows the namespaces they may list
# and says it was narrowed, an object is reviewed by its name, a log as
# `get pods/log`. Grant with ordinary Roles and RoleBindings;
# `kubectl auth can-i ... --as <user> --as-group <group>` answers what the
# console will. The ServiceAccount needs `create` on
# subjectaccessreviews.authorization.k8s.io for this, and never `impersonate`.
#
# **The answer is held for a minute, and that minute is a revocation delay**:
# removing a RoleBinding takes effect on a person's next write or read up to a
# minute after it is removed, not the moment it is. A review that cannot be made is
# held ten seconds and refuses - it is never read as a yes.
#
# The shared password account, where it is kept, is reviewed as the console's
# own ServiceAccount: it names nobody, so there is no person to ask about.
#
# **Not reviewed yet, so do not rely on it for them:**
#   - what an **extra** reads through its own clients (Host.Client): those are
#     the console's own clients, so a read through them is the ServiceAccount's
#     and is not asked about for the person. Its Host.List, Host.Usage and
#     Host.Forward are reviewed. **A write through those clients is refused
#     outright**, on every profile: an extra cannot change the cluster with
#     them, whoever is signed in.
#
# (On the desktop, where the request goes with the kubeconfig's own identity
# and the API server checks it, a review that cannot be made lets the request
# through and the line says decision=unreviewable. Under the server profile -
# here - it refuses, for a person and for the shared account alike.)

# The provider's identity, and the exact string every token's `iss` must equal.
# The browser is sent to the authorization endpoint its discovery names.
KT_OIDC_ISSUER=

# Where this process fetches discovery from, when it cannot reach the issuer's
# own address - a container reaching the provider by an internal name. The
# document must still name KT_OIDC_ISSUER, and the token and key-set endpoints
# are re-rooted here; the authorization endpoint is left for the browser.
# Empty means the issuer.
KT_OIDC_DISCOVERY_URL=

# The client registered at the provider. The secret may be empty for a public
# client; PKCE protects the exchange either way. The secret is never logged.
KT_OIDC_CLIENT_ID=
KT_OIDC_CLIENT_SECRET=

# This console's callback **as the browser reaches it**, ending in
# /api/auth/oidc/callback, and registered at the provider byte for byte. Not
# derived from a request, because a Host header is whatever the client wrote.
# Example: https://kubetower.example.com/api/auth/oidc/callback
KT_OIDC_REDIRECT_URL=

# Scopes, comma or space separated. Empty means `openid email profile`. Some
# providers emit the groups claim only when asked for `groups` (Dex does).
KT_OIDC_SCOPES=

# Which claim names the person, and which carries the groups. Empty means
# `email` and `groups`. Per provider, which is why they are settings. With
# `email`, a token saying email_verified=false is refused.
#
# **The name is the person's rights**: every action is reviewed for it, and the
# API server answers about whoever a RoleBinding names with that string. Use a
# claim the person cannot edit at the provider - `sub`, or an `email` the
# provider verifies. Not `preferred_username`, `name` or `upn`, and not an email
# the provider's users can set themselves: whoever they type is who they are.
KT_OIDC_USERNAME_CLAIM=
KT_OIDC_GROUPS_CLAIM=

# Prepended to the name before it is reviewed, logged and stored -
# kube-apiserver's --oidc-username-prefix, with its default: empty means the
# issuer followed by `#` (`https://idp.example/dex#CiQw...`), except for the
# `email` claim, which stays bare. `-` means no prefix at all, and has to be
# typed out. That default is the --oidc-* flags': a cluster configured through
# structured authentication has none, so set this to its
# claimMappings.username.prefix. RoleBindings name the prefixed string; the
# start-up log says which one. A person signed in under another prefix or claim
# signs in again. A name or prefix that would start with `system:`, or that carries
# a control character, is refused.
KT_OIDC_USERNAME_PREFIX=

# Prepended to every group before the console asserts it - kube-apiserver's
# --oidc-groups-prefix. A name, never a permission.
KT_OIDC_GROUPS_PREFIX=

# How long a single sign-on session lives from the sign-in, however much it is
# used. Default 12h, and KT_SESSION_MAX still applies where it is shorter. **It
# is also how long a group removed at the provider keeps being asserted**, since
# groups are read at sign-in - a revocation delay, not a convenience.
KT_OIDC_SESSION_MAX=12h

# Keep the shared password beside the provider. Off by default once an issuer
# is set: a shared password next to a provider is a way in that bypasses
# the provider, and nothing can say who used it.
KT_AUTH_LOCAL_ACCOUNT=


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Several replicas, and the store they share
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Where the single sign-on sessions and the confirmation plans live when this
# process is not the only one, as host:port. (A sign-in in progress is not
# here: it is a sealed cookie in the browser, which any replica sharing
# KT_SESSION_SECRET can open.)
# Empty keeps them in this process's memory, which is what one replica has
# always had. Valkey 7.2 or later; the Helm chart deploys one whenever
# replicaCount is above one. Every value is sealed with KT_SESSION_SECRET before
# it is written, so the store alone neither reads nor forges a session.
# **It keeps sessions across a restart only with a stable KT_SESSION_SECRET**:
# without one every start draws a new key, every cookie stops verifying, and
# the records already in the store are sealed under a key nobody holds.
KT_VALKEY_ADDR=
# The ACL user, empty for the default one, and its password. The password is
# never logged: the start-up line says only whether there is one.
KT_VALKEY_USERNAME=
KT_VALKEY_PASSWORD=

# true when this process is one of several behind one address. The process
# cannot see its own replica count, so the deployment says it. It refuses to
# start without KT_VALKEY_ADDR, without --incluster, without KT_SESSION_SECRET,
# and - where the shared password is offered - without KT_PASSWORD_HASH, because
# each of those would otherwise differ from pod to pod. And it takes away what
# only one pod can hold: the preference writes, the extension switches, the
# settings writes and port-forwards are not registered, and the archive is off.
KT_REPLICATED=


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Clusters
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Clusters where the terminal refuses mutating commands (comma-separated).
# Either the kubeconfig context name or the name that appears in a URL works.
#
# This is a guardrail against a mistyped command, not a trust boundary: the
# process still holds your credentials. For a real boundary, point the context
# at a read-only ServiceAccount.
#
#   KT_READONLY_CLUSTERS=prod-eks-1,prod-eks-2
KT_READONLY_CLUSTERS=

# Forces the "we are running in a container" verdict, which rewrites clusters
# declared on 127.0.0.1 to host.docker.internal and pins the TLS server name.
# Unset, it is detected from /.dockerenv, which is right in both directions —
# set it only to override that detection. A kind or k3d cluster reached from a
# container is the case that needs it. 1, true, yes or on force it; 0, false, no
# or off refuse it; any other value is logged and ignored, and detection decides.
# KT_IN_CONTAINER=1

# The cluster's Prometheus, as namespace/service:port - the Service port, e.g.
# monitoring/kube-prometheus-stack-prometheus:9090. It is what the charts' history
# is read from.
#
# **The server profile only** (--incluster), and there it is the whole choice:
# nothing is looked for, and unset means no Prometheus - the charts have no
# history and say why. It is never picked by labels: anybody who may create a
# Service may write those, and a tenant's Service would become the Prometheus
# every person's charts are drawn from, and receive every query. So the person
# who installs the console names it. With no --incluster the process refuses to
# start with this set: the desktop looks on each cluster of the kubeconfig
# itself, and one Service cannot name a Prometheus on all of them. compose.dev.yaml
# runs the desktop profile and does not forward it; the chart sets it.
KT_PROMETHEUS_SERVICE=

# The deployment's pin list for the additive pages: comma-separated keys, each
# optionally `=true` or `=false`, a bare key meaning true. Read at start-up
# only: restart KubeTower after changing it.
#
# The keys this build carries are `argocd` (the Delivery Center), `kubecron`
# (KubeCron) and `redis` (the Redis viewer), part of the console. Every extra
# is off until it is turned on, on every profile.
#
#   - On the desktop and in a container, what is set here **wins** over the
#     per-cluster switch (Settings -> Extra), which then refuses with a sentence
#     naming this variable.
#   - Under --incluster it pins nothing: it names the extras this installation
#     may turn on, and each stays off on a cluster until a person holding `use`
#     on it turns it on there (README, "Extras, and nothing held back").
#
# Leave it empty to let the per-cluster switch decide. An unknown key is a
# startup complaint naming the valid ones, not a silent no-op.
#
#   KT_EXTRAS=argocd,kubecron=false,redis
KT_EXTRAS=

# The deployment's pin list for the extras' own settings (the ones Settings ->
# Extra draws under each tile): comma-separated `<extra>.<setting>=on|off`, the
# same on/off spellings as KT_EXTRAS. A setting named here has that value on
# every cluster and every profile, the per-cluster write refuses it, and the
# tile draws it as pinned. Under --incluster this is the only way to change a
# setting, since the per-cluster write is not registered there. Read at
# start-up only. A name it cannot read is a startup complaint, never a refusal
# to start.
#
# The one setting this build offers is `argocd.list-column`: the Argo CD column
# in the resource lists, off by default.
#
#   KT_EXTRA_SETTINGS=argocd.list-column=on
KT_EXTRA_SETTINGS=

# Where the Delivery Center finds the read-only Argo CD token it shows Argo CD's
# own diff with, as `namespace/name`. The Secret's `token` key holds it; the
# value is sent to argocd-server through a tunnel and never to a browser.
# Unset, it is the Secret `kubetower-argocd-token` in the console's own
# namespace (`kubetower` outside a cluster). Without that Secret the Argo CD tab
# draws everything but Argo CD's diff, and says how to turn it on.
# KT_ARGOCD_TOKEN_SECRET=argocd/kubetower-argocd-token
#
# The same Secret's `ca.crt` key, when present, holds in PEM what argocd-server's
# certificate is verified against: the CA that signed it, or the certificate
# itself. Absent, the system's roots. A certificate that does not verify, or an
# argocd-server run with --insecure, gets no token: it is never sent over
# plain HTTP. A malformed `namespace/name` is said on the tab, never replaced
# by the default.
#
# The reliable setup: a certificate in the Secret `argocd-server-tls` (which
# argocd-server serves in preference to its own), issued by a CA you control,
# cert-manager for instance; that CA under `ca.crt`; and KT_ARGOCD_SERVER_NAME
# set to a name the certificate carries. Without `argocd-server-tls`, Argo CD
# generates a self-signed certificate once, keeps it as `tls.crt` in the Secret
# `argocd-secret` and serves it on every start. Pinning that `tls.crt` works,
# but the certificate is valid for a year and Argo CD does not renew it.

# argocd-server's Service, as `namespace/name`: the one Service Argo CD's token
# is sent to, through a tunnel. Unset, `argocd/argocd-server`, where Argo CD's
# upstream manifests put it, and a Helm release named `argocd` installed in
# `argocd`; a release named otherwise names it otherwise (`argo-cd` gives
# `argo-cd-argocd-server`). No Service is ever looked for by label.
# KT_ARGOCD_SERVER_SERVICE=argocd/argocd-server

# The name argocd-server's certificate is verified for. Unset, the Service's
# own DNS name, `<name>.<namespace>.svc`. Argo CD's self-signed certificate
# names `argocd-server`, `argocd-server.<namespace>` and
# `argocd-server.<namespace>.svc` whatever the Service is called, so a Service
# named otherwise needs this; so does a certificate issued for an ingress host.
# KT_ARGOCD_SERVER_NAME=argocd-server.argocd.svc

# Where KubeCron's collector is, and the Secret holding its token. The
# collector's chart protects its API with a token by default, and **the console
# reads that token only from the Secret named here and sends it only to the
# Service named here** - never from the Secret a Service's annotation names, since
# whoever may create or annotate a Service could otherwise choose which Secret
# the console reads with its own credentials, and receive it.
# KT_KUBECRON_COLLECTOR is `namespace/service`;
# KT_KUBECRON_TOKEN_SECRET is a Secret's name in that namespace (the chart's
# annotation `kubecron.io/auth-secret` on its Service says which), read only
# when that Service asks for a token; KT_KUBECRON_TOKEN_KEY is the key holding
# the token, `api-token` unset. The same names apply to every cluster: on a
# cluster where the named Service does not exist, and with nothing set, a
# collector is found by its `kubecron.io/collector` label and read without a
# token - one that asks for a token is not read, and the page says what to set.
# A value that is not a Kubernetes name is refused on the page. Read at
# start-up only.
# KT_KUBECRON_COLLECTOR=kubecron/kubecron
# KT_KUBECRON_TOKEN_SECRET=kubecron-api
# KT_KUBECRON_TOKEN_KEY=api-token


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The Claude control
#
# It runs on the operator's own subscription, authenticated once from inside
# the terminal. There is no API key in this project and no model call leaves
# that plan. The image carries it only when built with CLAUDE_CODE_VERSION set.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Whether Claude sessions see the read-only overrides on **every** cluster
# rather than only those in KT_READONLY_CLUSTERS. Defaults to true, and it
# should stay true unless you mean it: a model asked to fix a workload will run
# `kubectl delete` to fix it.
#
# The override is measured rather than asserted — a `kubectl` shim in front of
# the real one on the session's path, written only if the server could find
# kubectl at start-up. A session marked read-only whose shim is measured absent
# says so in its own pane and starts in `manual` mode whatever the variable
# below says. A guardrail believed and absent is worse than one absent and known.
#
# Set to false, a session may write but still does not read Secrets: its
# `kubectl` refuses every spelling of a Secret read and every kubectl plugin
# reached through kubectl (krew's `view-secret` among them, and `kubectl krew`
# itself), since a plugin can read a Secret by a door no pattern names. A plugin
# binary called by its own name (`kubectl-view_secret x`) never meets the
# override: a guardrail, not a boundary. A human shell is not affected. With
# false the session's kubectl also keeps, among others, `exec`, `attach`, `cp`
# and `debug`, so it can read what is mounted in a pod, Secrets included.
# KT_CLAUDE_READONLY=true

# The permission mode a Claude session starts in. Defaults to `auto`.
# Accepted: auto, acceptEdits, dontAsk, manual, plan. `bypassPermissions` is
# refused — the dial for a session that may change things is KT_CLAUDE_READONLY
# above, which takes the shims away rather than the questions.
# KT_CLAUDE_PERMISSION_MODE=auto

# The pinned Claude Code version, read by `compose.dev.yaml` as a build
# argument. Empty builds an image without Claude Code at all.
# CLAUDE_CODE_VERSION=2.1.226


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# KubeTower's own state
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Where the hotbar arrangement, the table column layouts, the credentials file
# and the archive live. `compose.dev.yaml` sets it to /var/lib/kubetower and
# mounts a named volume there, which is what makes the rail survive a rebuild.
# Outside a container it defaults to ~/.kubetower.
#
# Deliberately disposable — nothing in it is a source of truth — **except the
# credentials file**, which holds the drawn password's hash and the session key.
# Delete the volume and the next start draws a new password.
#
# For `task dev`, `task dev:password` sets it to `~/.kubetower-dev` (written out
# as an absolute path) when it is empty, so the development loop never writes
# into the `~/.kubetower` of the KubeTower you actually use. Compose does not
# read it from here: `compose.dev.yaml` sets the container's own. `task dev`
# reads it literally - no `${USERPROFILE}`, no `~` - and refuses a value that
# is not an absolute path rather than create the directory inside the clone.
# KT_STATE_DIR=/var/lib/kubetower

# Whether that directory survives a restart, **as the deployment says it** -
# the console cannot tell an emptyDir from a volume, both answer every write.
# Unset is persistent, which is every desktop. `false` takes away what could not
# outlive the process: the archive is not opened and its settings, and the
# node-shell image setting, are absent from the Settings page and from the API
# rather than offered and forgotten. The rail, the columns and the names still
# work, for as long as the process runs. The Helm chart writes it from
# `persistence.enabled` on every install.
# KT_STATE_PERSISTENT=true

# The archive: a kept window of cluster events and, optionally, pod logs.
# Kubernetes forgets — events go after about an hour and a pod's logs go with
# the pod, which is exactly when they are wanted.
#
# KT_ARCHIVE defaults to on. KT_ARCHIVE_LOGS defaults to off, and read this
# before turning it on: logs are production application output written to the
# state volume unredacted and unencrypted — whatever an application printed,
# tokens and personal data included, readable by anyone with the Docker socket.
# That is a data-retention decision, not a console preference.
# KT_ARCHIVE=true
# KT_ARCHIVE_LOGS=false

# Days kept, clamped to 1–90. Default 7.
# KT_ARCHIVE_RETENTION_DAYS=7

# Whether the desktop application asks GitHub, once a day at most, whether a
# newer release exists. Defaults to on; `0`, `false`, `no` or `off` turns it off,
# and anything else leaves it on rather than guessing.
#
# **Turned off, nothing is requested at all** — the switch is read before a
# socket is opened, not after the answer comes back. Left on, it is one
# unauthenticated GET of a public JSON document: no query string, no instance
# identifier, no cluster name, and no version in the User-Agent. It offers; it
# never downloads and never installs.
#
# The answer and the time it was asked go in `update.json` beside the rest of the
# state, so a restart loop makes one request a day rather than one per start.
# A console left open asks again once that answer is a day old (an hour after a
# failure), and Settings › Infos has a **Check now** that asks past the day; off,
# neither makes a request either.
#
# **It does nothing in the container.** A console running in a cluster is
# upgraded by its image tag and never checks, whatever this says — which is why
# `compose.dev.yaml` deliberately does not pass it.
# KT_UPDATE_CHECK=true


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# EKS credentials
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# An EKS context authenticates through the `exec` block in the kubeconfig, and
# that block is `aws eks get-token`. What it returns is not a secret the CLI
# holds: it is a presigned STS GetCallerIdentity URL. KubeTower signs the same
# URL in this process, so no `aws` is started at all — which is why the image
# opens no console window per token on Windows and writes nothing into the
# assume-role cache two contexts could corrupt.
#
# Set KT_AWS_EXEC to anything and every EKS context runs `aws eks get-token`
# again, exactly as before. It exists because in-process signing replaced a
# credential path that worked for years, and a way back that does not need a new
# build is worth one variable.
# KT_AWS_EXEC=1

# A read-only re-mount of the **host's** `~/.aws/cli/cache`, so the console's
# home can name a corrupt entry — a failure whose entire error message is a
# 40-character hash, and which breaks the `aws` and `kubectl` in the operator's
# own terminal rather than anything in this container. Read-only on purpose: the
# console must be able to name the file to delete and must never become one more
# writer of the directory whose concurrent writers are the cause.
#
# Set by `compose.dev.yaml`; there is nothing to choose, because it names a path
# inside the container. **Unset is correct on the host** — there the process's
# own cache already is the operator's, and it is scanned as such.
#   KT_HOST_AWS_CACHE=/host-aws-cache


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The node shell
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# A node cannot be exec'd into, so opening a shell on one creates a pod on that
# node — host PID, network and IPC, with the node's root filesystem at /host.
# Closing the terminal deletes it. It is refused on a cluster in
# KT_READONLY_CLUSTERS.
#
# This is the image that pod runs, and it is the **default** only: what is set
# on the Settings page is stored in KT_STATE_DIR and wins. The image needs
# /bin/sh and nothing else, and the **node's** container runtime is what pulls
# it — so on an air-gapped cluster this wants to be your own registry.
#
# Unset, it is alpine:3.24 — the image this repository's Dockerfile builds from,
# so every build has already pulled it — **pinned by the digest of its
# multi-arch index** (internal/nodeshell, DefaultImage), because the pod is
# privileged and a tag is whatever the registry answers on the day. A value set
# here is used as written: pin it by digest too.
# KT_NODE_SHELL_IMAGE=registry.example.com/debug/alpine:3.24@sha256:<digest>


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The launch report
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# On every start, while this is a 0.x build, KubeTower writes about thirty lines
# to the log describing **this machine**: the executable, the arguments, the
# window decision and why, PATH and which of kubectl/helm/aws/claude resolve on
# it, what is in the state directory, and which of the variables this process
# reads are set. A colleague who hits something and sends their log should be
# sending the diagnosis with it.
#
# No value that could be a credential is in it: a variable's value is printed
# only if its name is on a closed allowlist in `internal/launch/diagnostics.go`,
# every other variable that is set is **named** and not printed, and a second
# guard refuses any name that reads as a secret even if somebody allowlists it.
#
# The gate is the version, so the decision expires on its own.
#   KT_DIAG=off   quiet on a 0.x build
#   KT_DIAG=on    verbose on a 1.x build
# KT_DIAG=


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Set by compose.dev.yaml, listed here so nothing looks like it is missing
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
#   KUBECONFIG=/home/kubetower/.kube/config
#   AWS_CONFIG_FILE=/home/kubetower/.aws/config
#   AWS_SHARED_CREDENTIALS_FILE=/home/kubetower/.aws/credentials
#   CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR=/home/kubetower/.claude
#
# **The AWS pair is read by KubeTower itself**, not only inherited by an `aws`
# binary: `aws-sdk-go-v2` in `internal/ekstoken` reads both, so they decide
# which credentials the server authenticates with. They deliberately do not move
# the SSO token cache, which is always `<home>/.aws/sso/cache` and derived from
# no variable at all — so pointing AWS_CONFIG_FILE at another directory
# separates a profile from its own SSO session.
#
# **KUBECONFIG is a list, not a path**, and it is here rather than above for
# that reason: it names one or more files separated by `os.PathListSeparator` —
# `;` on Windows, `:` everywhere else, including inside the container — merged
# the way kubectl merges them. Set it in the compose file, not here, because
# those paths are the container's:
#
#   KUBECONFIG=/home/kubetower/.kube/config:/home/kubetower/.kube/second.yaml
#
# On the host it is your own, with your own separator, and KubeTower reads it
# with `filepath.SplitList` rather than splitting on a character somebody's
# machine happened to use.
#
# Several files need no new mount: HOST_KUBE_DIR is mounted as a whole
# directory, so anything dropped in it is already there. Keep it that way — a
# bind mount of a single **file** arrives as an empty directory, silently, from
# a repository on a network share.
#
# Three behaviors worth knowing, all of them kubectl's rather than ours:
#
#   * on a name two files declare, the **first file wins** and the other entry
#     is discarded whole — so two files each holding a `prod` context give one
#     `prod`, and the other exists nowhere. KubeTower says so at startup,
#     because nothing else about it is visible;
#   * a path that is **not there** is skipped, so naming a file that only exists
#     on another machine is harmless;
#   * a path that **is** there and does not parse refuses the start, and the
#     message names the file.
